[["\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 45294 and 96404?\n\n# Reasoning:\n\nLet's calculate the product of **45,294** and **96,404** step by step.\n\n1. **Multiply 45,294 by 4 (ones place of 96,404):**\n   \\[\n   45,\\!294 \\times 4 = 181,\\!176\n   \\]\n\n2. **Multiply 45,294 by 0 (tens place of 96,404):**\n   \\[\n   45,\\!294 \\times 0 = 0\n   \\]\n   *(Note: This step results in 0 and does not affect the total sum.)*\n\n3. **Multiply 45,294 by 400 (hundreds place of 96,404):**\n   \\[\n   45,\\!294 \\times 400 = 18,\\!117,\\!600\n   \\]\n\n4. **Multiply 45,294 by 6,000 (thousands place of 96,404):**\n   \\[\n   45,\\!294 \\times 6,\\!000 = 271,\\!764,\\!000\n   \\]\n\n5. **Multiply 45,294 by 90,000 (ten-thousands place of 96,404):**\n   \\[\n   45,\\!294 \\times 90,\\!000 = 4,\\!076,\\!460,\\!000\n   \\]\n\n# Answer:\n\nNow, sum all the step results:\n\\[\n181,\\!176 + 0 + 18,\\!117,\\!600 + 271,\\!764,\\!000 + 4,\\!076,\\!460,\\!000 = 4,\\!366,\\!513,\\!776\n\\]\n\nSo, the final computed product is \\(\\boxed{4,\\!366,\\!513,\\!776}\\).", "\nFirst, I need to calculate the product of 45,294 and 96,404. To make this manageable, I'll break down the multiplication into smaller, more straightforward steps.\n\nI'll start by multiplying 45,294 by each digit of 96,404, taking into account the place value of each digit. This means I'll handle the ones place, tens place, hundreds place, thousands place, and ten-thousands place separately.\n\nAfter obtaining each partial product, I'll add them all together to get the final result. This step-by-step approach ensures accuracy and makes the multiplication process more organized.\n"]]
